{"kind":"report","text":"Independent design review of exact 6ecfa122a0088004f57a7106fcab3a364022da9c, all three artifacts, primary193162d7, implementation10fcfe4e: changes requested. Recording every request before sending it, separate retry records, atomic pushToken ownership transfer, retaining unknown effects and never sweeping shared canonical inventory are sound. Repair these contract and lane acceptance gaps before adoption; this review does not ask for all three implementing lanes now.\n\nArchitecture: The affected layers are Room's SQLite/alarms and check-job ownership, Git's landing/publication and remote-effect ledgers, and Publisher/log/snapshot adapters. The changed contract is published in docs/protocol.md at this same head, with a separate lane plan. Keeping job_tokens and plan003 ownership rather than moving them into a new ledger reduces churn. Production source is unchanged from3ac55e96. A design approval would authorize no live operation; follow-on work needs its own effective request and adopted decision provenance. The authority-bearing-chain four facts must be established before follow-on implementation approval, including satisfied/merged decision and sufficient requester authority; they are not all established by this unmerged draft.\n\nSecurity: Attributed ID-only revocation and never treating elapsed time or inventory as a completion fence protect unrelated owners. The current response classification nevertheless discards a directly returned ID when token text is missing, and its literal unknown-settlement rule misses a later conclusive answer. The wake policy also leaves durable observation dependent on an unrelated next invocation. Repair both as below. Keep lifetimes unchanged and distinguish the assumed provider expiry semantics from measured facts.\n\nSimplification: Keep one ownership state machine and one shared observation snapshot rather than copying the same count onto every lifetime-retained row. Preserve all records and usable functionality. Limits should bound each processing turn and control new work explicitly, not delete unresolved effects.\n\n1. [P1] Store a recovery wake before the remote effect, or specify and prove an equivalent already-stored wake (note:180-190; protocol Scheduling:3336-3339; laneB test5). The note explicitly stores no wake before create and relies on 'the next instance' to take over after the host stops. A stored SQLite intent does not itself invoke a DO: room.ts:recover only runs when the object is instantiated, and core.ts:nextAlarm can return null when unrelated work is idle. If the last relevant alarm runs while this host still holds the record, the design excludes that held/sent work from nextDue, so a subsequent stop can leave no alarm. Known tokens eventually expire, but that is not recovery/observation of the unknown record or fulfillment of R-MINT-5's admin-visible continuing duty. Persist a future takeover/observation wake before sending, reusing any existing earlier alarm through Room.wake's read-before-set behavior. A failed wake must send nothing. Maintain future recovery while a call is outstanding; expiry never settles an unknown create. Add real-DO controls for no pre-existing alarm, crash/lost answer, and the earlier alarm firing before the host stops; verify stored scheduling and bounded nextDue without another user request.\n\n2. [P2] Separate usable token text from attributable token ID, and allow a conclusive late answer to resolve the original request (note:115-122, laneA test4; R-MINT-3:3284-3295 and R-MINT-5:3314-3318). The test explicitly requires an answer without text to remain unknown. If a matching create response returns a valid ID but missing/invalid plaintext, the caller cannot use the token, but the provider has directly attributed its ID to this request and revokeToken accepts that ID. Preserve the ID in durable owed revocation rather than losing it behind an indefinitely observed unknown row. With malformed expiry, retain null/unknown expiry and retry by ID; do not invent an expiry that closes the debt. Similarly, 'only a provider completion fence can settle an unknown' must also allow the original request's later attributable answer or a definite unchanged refusal. Such an answer proves this request's outcome; an inventory/time guess does not. Add held-reply/restart controls that reject use by a stale caller while atomically recording/revoking its returned ID, preserve the old intent if the handoff commit fails, and ensure old completions cannot delete another owner's row. No unrelated inventory token is selected.\n\n3. [P2] Bound actual observation and database work, not just the count of inventory calls (note:162-190; laneA test7; R-MINT-5 and Scheduling). The note keeps unknown records for the Room's lifetime and writes the same timestamp/count onto every due row. One inventory for N rows still performs O(N) repeated writes, and querying/materializing every known ID/inventory/duty can grow without bound. The claim that cost grows 'only ... in storage' is false for the prescribed writes, scans and reads. Repeated new unknowns resetting the schedule to5seconds can repeatedly revisit the retained history. Use a shared observation record plus bounded indexed batches/cursors, or another explicit bounded design; budget provider wait/response size and incomplete inventories without settling anything. Preserve fairness for earlier owed revocations and observations when new mints arrive. Add scaled controls with many retained unknowns/known IDs and continually arriving new unknowns that assert bounded per-turn rows/writes/awaits, forward cursor progress and a non-spinning durable wake. Duties must support bounded reads for the existing cleanup projection; no FIFO loss of unknown effects.\n\n4. [P2] Narrow and update the provider evidence (note:69-89, protocol intro/open points). The repository's TokenInfo structural type is a local subset, not proof of all provider capabilities. Current Cloudflare REST documentation does expose created_at in token listings (https://developers.cloudflare.com/artifacts/api/rest-api/, checked2026-10-02), while the Workers-binding page documents no creation-time field (https://developers.cloudflare.com/artifacts/api/workers-binding/). Correct the 'only the in-memory fake' claim and distinguish REST from the used binding. Creation time still does not establish ownership among concurrent same-scope mints, so the no-guess/no-sweep decision remains correct. Say no attribution/fence is documented in the examined interfaces rather than claiming a global negative from absence in local types. R-MINT-6 must label its apply-time lifetime bound as an assumption adjacent to the guarantee, and note:137-141 must schedule unknown observation as well as owed revocation on recovery. Use the currently active cleanup-projection request (or its explicit successor) and preserve a tracked handoff for the parallel fork-token defect; the superseded c0 request is historical provenance, not a current assignment.\n\nValidation: clean exact clone /tmp/artroom-checker-mint-v1; read the entire359-line design note, complete112-line protocol amendment and plans pointer, all three exact artifact statements, invitation/promise and governing10fcfe4e/9f2d8808/4efb1ef3 records. Reopened production mint/retry sites, landing ownership gap, job mint classification, Room alarm recovery and nullable nextAlarm, and current primary Artifacts REST/Workers-binding docs. git diff --check passes, tracked status is clean, and only the three documentation files differ from3ac55e96. This head adds no runtime guard, so no mutation tests or fresh runtime-suite/provider-call claims. The listed controls are required for the future implementation, not tests claimed to run here.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:193162d70644c7a7f51a24527953127c7525ff1e","binding":"assigned","head":"6ecfa122a0088004f57a7106fcab3a364022da9c","head_news_acknowledged":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:852a2b2cd32f11ce4ec1670cdd0104af7a914bec\"]","implementations":"[\"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:a6e2c3de639d14f85772b4255a59954169024daa\"]","review_frontier":"git:sha1:589bca982736a2361d15d53f7572375ef2f7aaf3#git:sha1:84f5c24cecc541e2d81cbea5404462180e36f9fc","review_path":"reviewguard@1","verdict":"changes-requested"}}